{
  "book": "GEN",
  "chapter": 23,
  "resources": {
    "costumbres": {
      "c-43c2a02c33747b61": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-25",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "Abraham does not go directly to Ephron, but he gets some of the Hittites to plead for him. No business of importance can to this day be transacted in the East without middlemen.",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-25",
          "sourceHash": "db933abe311ff2f0b16379ae490d8645e36bc0a65021204fd42923f6b7482d3f",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      },
      "c-5145eeba833fb6e4": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-23",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "We shall have occasion, in noticing other passages, to refer to the different modes of manifesting grief at times of bereavement; it is only necessary to say here, that there is in this text an evident allusion to a ceremonial mourning. The word “came” indicates this. The passage…",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-23",
          "sourceHash": "5abd643b4864710a0ab94232bced24f0095a2d52965ff47babd5a1d3c55269e9",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      },
      "c-5dcaf65dc88364d3": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-26",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "Abraham hearkened unto Ephron; and Abraham weighed to Ephron the silver, which he had named in the audience of the sons of Heth, four hundred shekels of silver, current money with the merchant.",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-26",
          "sourceHash": "d66f9506a054615c26a0a13364141e227b4b25e12eaa53b84674348f5ebbd5e6",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      },
      "c-715d2eff4da65c0e": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-24",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "We have in the interesting narrative of this business transaction an exact representation of the Oriental mode of trafficking. Abraham, a great prince, but a stranger, wishes to buy a piece of land for a family burial place. He makes the proposition to those members of the tribe…",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-24",
          "sourceHash": "7d5b6ab81d15b61bbefecd7f5a6f46ef84fa41b53d863f0a70b170a0865fec76",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      },
      "c-bad11392a0205be1": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-28",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "Sepulchral caves are still found in many parts of the East. Sometimes a natural cave is used, with such modifications as necessity may require. The place where Abraham buried Sarah was undoubtedly a natural cave. Tombs were frequently hewn out of the rock. See note on Isaiah 22:1…",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-28",
          "sourceHash": "b99b713612abf8477fcde62a43a47dc8667081605c087d7f241df5c036a2b7e9",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      },
      "c-c2e120d1626a5b72": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-27",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "The field of Ephron, which was in Machpelah, which was before Mamre, the field, and the cave which was therein, and all the trees that were in the field, that were in all the borders round about, were made sure unto Abraham for a possession in the presence of the children of Heth…",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-27",
          "sourceHash": "8b6f6f27e71626c61f754f7ccbede63179c12e1ded570c7cda1febebe66464cd",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      }
    },
